1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,975
This is Data Leak Guard: it keeps Indian personal data and secrets
from reaching an LLM.

2
00:00:07,075 --> 00:00:13,006
Your app keeps its code. You change one line, the base URL, so
requests go through the guard.

3
00:00:14,106 --> 00:00:20,122
Here is a support message with a name, an Aadhaar number, a UPI ID, a
phone number and a home address.

4
00:00:20,472 --> 00:00:27,555
This is what actually left the machine: every one of them is now a
placeholder, so the model never sees the real values.

5
00:00:27,905 --> 00:00:36,054
The reply comes back with the real values put back in, before your app
sees it. This was recorded live against a real model on Groq.

6
00:00:37,154 --> 00:00:42,402
Streaming works too. Here is a short request whose reply is streamed
back piece by piece.

7
00:00:42,752 --> 00:00:46,229
On the wire, the model's placeholder arrives split across chunks.

8
00:00:46,579 --> 00:00:52,915
The guard holds back only what could still become a placeholder, so
your app gets the real number, still streamed.

9
00:00:54,015 --> 00:00:58,538
Now a developer pastes a config file that contains a database
password.

10
00:00:58,888 --> 00:01:03,133
The guard blocks the whole request with an error, and nothing is sent
to the model.

11
00:01:04,233 --> 00:01:11,550
Not every number is sensitive: an OTP that happens to be a real PIN
code, and a twelve-digit order number.

12
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:15,100
Both go through unchanged, so the model can still help.

13
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:23,517
For every request, the guard logs what it found and restored as
counts. The values themselves never appear in its logs.

14
00:01:24,617 --> 00:01:31,081
How good is the detection? Here is Microsoft Presidio on the same
held-out test set, set up two ways.

15
00:01:31,431 --> 00:01:38,727
The guard has a higher F1 than both setups, with far fewer false
alarms on messages with nothing sensitive in them.

16
00:01:39,827 --> 00:01:45,224
To try it, run docker compose up. No API key is needed for the
built-in mock model.

17
00:01:45,574 --> 00:01:53,766
One honest limit: UPI IDs with handles it doesn't know are often
missed. The README has the full results and the threat model.
